# Copy to `env`, fill in, then run ./install.sh user@router
# The installer copies this to /root/finn/env with mode 0600.

# --- required -----------------------------------------------------------------
# From @BotFather in Telegram: /newbot, then paste the token it gives you.
FINN_TELEGRAM_TOKEN=123456789:AAF...

# Your own numeric Telegram id. Ask @userinfobot for it. He answers nobody else.
FINN_OWNER_ID=

# What he calls you. He talks to exactly one person and it may as well be by name.
FINN_OWNER_NAME=

# One of these two. A dedicated key with a low spend limit is strongly advised: it sits in
# plaintext on a device other people share a network with.
#   Anthropic: https://console.anthropic.com  ->  API keys
#   OpenAI:    https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
FINN_ANTHROPIC_KEY=sk-ant-...
#FINN_OPENAI_KEY=sk-...

# --- which brain --------------------------------------------------------------
FINN_PROVIDER=anthropic          # anthropic | openai
FINN_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5       # or gpt-5.5 with FINN_PROVIDER=openai
# A small model is a false economy here. Tested on haiku: it answered about the wrong
# machine and let the metaphor swallow the fact. He speaks a few times a day; pay for it.

# --- who lives here -----------------------------------------------------------
# DHCP hostnames, not MAC addresses: phones rotate their MAC, hostnames survive it.
# Look them up with: cat /tmp/dhcp.leases
FINN_DESK_HOST=iMac              # the machine you sit at
FINN_PHONE_HOST=iPhone           # the thing you carry, so he can tell you are here
FINN_LAPTOP_HOST=Mac             # anything else you want him to know by name

# Everything else that lives here and is not a person: printers, a NAS, a doorbell. Without
# this he counts them as strangers and grumbles about them nightly. Quote it, it has spaces.
#FINN_KNOWN_HOSTS="NPIEE33CD=the office printer, NAS=the file box"

# People you know, by the device they carry. He will tell you when they arrive and leave,
# and he will suggest you go and say hello. Matched on DHCP hostname, which stays put even
# when the phone randomises its MAC on your SSID. Run `tick.sh who` to see what to write here.
# This is for people on YOUR network who know it exists. It is not a tool for identifying
# strangers on a shared building network, and it is not built to be one.
#FINN_PEOPLE="Johns-iPhone=John, Kamilla-MacBook=Kamilla"

# --- your hardware ------------------------------------------------------------
# Defaults suit a GL.iNet GL-MT3000. Check yours with: iwinfo ; ip link
FINN_WAN_IFACE=eth0              # the interface facing the internet
FINN_RADIOS=ra0,rax0             # your access point interfaces, comma separated

# --- optional -----------------------------------------------------------------
# An interface facing a wider network you are a guest on, an office or building wifi you
# repeat from. Gives him a world beyond the wall: strangers arriving, the crowd swelling.
# Leave empty if this router is the top of your network.
FINN_UPSTREAM_IFACE=

# Machines he can wake and sleep from the bot with /wake and /sleep.
#   name = MAC / ip / ssh-user     (ip and ssh-user optional)
# Wake sends a WOL magic packet (needs the machine wired, or genuinely WoWLAN-capable --
# a sleeping Mac on plain WiFi usually will not wake, that is an Apple limitation, not this).
# Sleep runs an ssh command on the machine itself, over /root/.ssh/id_mac, so the machine
# needs Remote Login on and that key in authorized_keys. Leave the user off and only /wake works.
#FINN_MACHINES="mac=d2:4c:e1:3c:31:01/192.168.8.167/yuri"
#FINN_WOL_IFACE=br-lan

# Fleet watchdog: the router is outside your server's blast radius, so it is the thing that
# still notices when the server dies. Names mapped to a host (pinged) or an http(s) url
# (fetched, 2xx/3xx = up). A target must miss FINN_WATCH_FAILS checks before he calls it down,
# and an outage overrides quiet hours and the daily budget -- it is the one thing worth waking
# you for at 3am.
# A third target shape, proxy:<url>, watches an egress path rather than a host: he fetches
# through that proxy and needs a real address back. FINN_EGRESS_IS makes it stricter -- the
# address has to be that one, so a proxy that answers from somewhere unexpected counts as
# down rather than up. Useful when something downstream depends on traffic leaving by a
# particular line, and "the proxy is running" is not the same question.
#FINN_WATCH="server=203.0.113.10, app=https://app.example.com, uk proxy=proxy:http://10.88.0.2:8888"
#FINN_WATCH_FAILS=3
#FINN_EGRESS_IS=198.51.100.7

# Congestion alarm: gateway latency held above FINN_CONGEST_MS for FINN_CONGEST_MINS minutes
# is the signature of a saturated shared link. Fired as an urgent (fleet-grade) alert that
# reaches you past quiet hours. Baseline on a healthy wired link is well under 1ms.
#FINN_CONGEST_MS=15
#FINN_CONGEST_MINS=5

# How long a phone must be unseen on the wifi before it counts as "gone" (seconds). Wi-Fi
# association flaps constantly; without this, a phone in a pocket looks like it leaves and
# returns every few minutes and the arrival greeting never gets an honest away-duration.
#FINN_PRESENCE_GONE_S=300

# Speech: which OpenAI voice, and volume. Many cheap USB speakers ignore the ALSA PCM control
# (it reports a level and plays at full anyway), so volume is done in software by an ALSA
# softvol device — see asound.conf, copy it to /etc/asound.conf. FINN_VOLUME sets that.
#FINN_TTS_VOICE=echo
#FINN_VOLUME=72

# Wireguard interfaces, if you have them. The first is a tunnel out that he can notice
# going quiet; the second is your own VPN, so he can feel someone climbing in.
FINN_TUNNEL_IFACE=
FINN_VPN_IFACE=
